# Why do we buy what we buy?
voices: {"a":"gemini:Achird | curious, open-minded economist host who loves a puzzle; warm, thinks aloud, brisk lively pace (never slow, never rushed); takes objections seriously, never preachy","b":"gemini:Kore | sharp, friendly co-host: asks what a smart listener would, pushes back with real objections, sometimes explains; dry wit, never exaggerated; brisk lively pace, never slow"}
prompt: "What does economics assume about what people want? Why does it refuse to judge tastes?"
next: {"title":"What does \"rational\" mean to an economist?","href":"https://www.drawcast.app/#gh=hmelberg/drawcast-library/econ-from-zero/72-what-does-rational-mean-to-an-economist.cast"}

## A $6.2 million banana
sources:
    - id: npr2024
      title: A duct-taped banana sells for $6.2 million at an art auction
      authors: NPR
      year: 2024
      finding: Maurizio Cattelan's Comedian sold at Sotheby's New York on 20 November 2024 for $6.2 million with fees ($5.2 million hammer) to Justin Sun.
      url: https://www.npr.org/2024/11/21/nx-s1-5199568/a-duct-taped-banana-sells-for-6-2-million-at-an-art-auction
    - id: tan2024
      title: Justin Sun eats Cattelan banana he bought for $6.2m
      authors: The Art Newspaper
      year: 2024
      finding: The buyer ate the banana on camera; the work is sold as a certificate of authenticity with instructions to replace the banana.
      url: https://www.theartnewspaper.com/2024/11/29/justin-sun-eats-catellan-banana-he-bought-for-62m

