    card title "Why do we buy what we buy?"

Why do we buy what we buy? In November 2024, someone bought this: a banana, duct-taped to a wall.
    draw frame banana tape

B: At Sotheby's in New York. The price, with fees: (@draw price@) six point two million dollars.

    pause 0.4

The buyer, the cry (@draw buyer@)pto entrepreneur Justin Sun, later peeled the  (@erase banana@)banana and ate it on camera.

B: Which didn't matter much. What he had real (@draw cert lab_cert@)ly bought was a certificate: the right to tape up a fresh bana (@draw banana2@)na whenever the old one goes brown.

So, before any theory: was he being foolish?
    highlight price effect underline

B: My gut says yes, obviously.
    highlight banana2

    quiz "Was paying $6.2 million for the banana foolish?" judge false
        right: Hold on to your answer.
        * Yes, obviously
        * No, it's an investment
        * Economics won't say

(@erase cert lab_cert buyer@) Most economists would refuse t (@draw verdict@)o answer, and not to dodge. That refusal is what this lecture is about.

It also opens a run of lectures on one question:  (@draw chip1@)how do people choose? What they want, wha (@draw chip2@)t they can afford, and whether they choo (@draw chip3@)se as sensibly as the models say.

The refusal ma (@highlight verdict@)tters. Every claim that a market or a tax makes people better or worse off counts their wants as they  (@highlight banana2@)are, banana and all.

B: So what does economics assume people want?
    erase chips

Surprisingly little. Only that they can r (@draw rank lab_rank@)ank things: offered two options, they prefer one, or they don't mind which.

Sun's bid shows (@morph rank tex "\\text{banana} \\succ \\$6.2\\text{ million}"@) his ranking: this banana above six point two million dollars of anything else. Whether for art, fame or  (@highlight rank@)a joke, the model doesn't ask.

## Choices you can see
