    scratch util work [{"tex":"U(\\text{sourdough}) > U(\\text{white})"},{"tex":"2 > 1 \\quad 20 > 10 \\quad 1000 > 1"},"same order = same preferences","ordinal: order, not amount"] x 500 y 420 font_size 28 hidden true

    card title "Choices you can see"

Ranking is all economics assumes about wants. But nobody can see a ranking inside someone's head.
    draw head

B: So how would you ever read it?
    draw headq

From choices. That was Paul Samue (@draw samuelson@)lson's idea in 1938: what you pick, when you could have had somethi (@draw rp@)ng else, reveals what you prefer.
    erase head headq

To see how, take Mia's bakery in Harbourtown, with illu (@draw axes@)strative numbers: the most each of four regulars would pay for her sourdough loaf.
    erase samuelson rp

B: Ann (@draw bar_1 why_box why_line_1 why_line_2@)a would pay nine dollars, for  (@draw bar_2 why_line_3@)the taste. Ben seven, to  (@draw bar_3 why_line_4@)impress his dinner guests. Cara five, as a gift for her mother. Dev th (@draw bar_4 why_line_5 ends@)ree.

Mia ch (@draw reference_1@)arges six fifty. Anna a (@highlight bar_1 bar_2@)nd Ben buy; Cara and Dev walk past.

Mia never learns their reasons, and she doesn't need to: the taste, the showing off and t (@highlight bar_1 bar_2 bar_3@)he gift all arrive as one number each.

Now Mia cuts her price (@draw reference_2@) to four fifty.
    erase why reference_1

    ask "At $4.50, who is the new buyer? Tap their bar." choose ["bar_1","bar_2","bar_3","bar_4"] answer bar_3
        right: Cara: she'd pay up to five, and the price is now four fifty.
        wrong: A buyer buys when the most they'd pay is at least the price: here, Cara.

B: Now say Ben's guests stop being impressed by sourdough. His number drops to three, and he stops buying.
    animate stage 1 duration 2.5

Nobody had to rule on whether showing off is a good reason. The choice changed, and the model only needed the choice.
    highlight bar_2

Line the bars up from highest to  (@draw demand@)lowest and you have a demand curve: wants, t (@draw lab_demand@)urned into what people would pay.

So economics can stay silent about tastes and still say a lot: it works with the numbers that choices leave behind.
    highlight demand

    draw mathq

    ask "Show the math, or skip it?" on mathq stage own

@math

    erase mathq

    fade axes bar_1 bar_2 bar_3 bar_4 reference_2 demand lab_demand to 0.15

Economists write a ranking as a utility function, U: a bigger number for whatever you prefer.
    draw util_box util_line_1

B: Give sourdough a U of 2 a (@draw util_line_2 util_line_3@)nd white bread 1. Or 20 and 10. Or 1000 and 1. The order is the same, so the preferences are the same.

So utility measures order, not how much happier you are. That's why it can't (@highlight util_line_4@) weigh your joy in a banana against someone else's.
    draw util_line_4

    erase util

@test

    erase mathq

    fade axes bar_1 bar_2 bar_3 bar_4 reference_2 demand lab_demand to 1

## Who made the wants?
sources:
    - id: galbraith1958
      title: The Affluent Society
      authors: John Kenneth Galbraith
      year: 1958
      finding: 'The ''dependence effect'': wants increasingly depend on the process of production that satisfies them, through emulation and advertising, so their urgency cannot justify the urgency of production.'
    - id: hayek1961
      title: The Non Sequitur of the 'Dependence Effect'
      authors: Friedrich A. Hayek
      year: 1961
      finding: Nearly all wants beyond the most basic are learned from one's culture; that a want is acquired does not make it unimportant.
    - id: stiglerbecker1977
      title: De Gustibus Non Est Disputandum
      authors: George J. Stigler and Gary S. Becker
      year: 1977
      finding: 'Tastes can be treated as stable and similar across people; differences in behaviour are explained by prices, incomes and accumulated ''consumption capital'' (e.g. music appreciation). American Economic Review 67(2): 76–90.'

    box method "Method" x 260 y 570 width 320 height 80 radius 12 font_size 32 hidden true
    box respect "Respect" x 740 y 570 width 320 height 80 radius 12 font_size 32 hidden true
    text sub_method "choices are the data" #8f887c x 260 y 495 font_size 26 hidden true
    text sub_respect "you judge your own life" #8f887c x 740 y 495 font_size 26 hidden true
    box c1 "Galbraith 1958: firms make wants" cites ["galbraith1958"] x 330 y 400 width 560 height 66 radius 10 font_size 26 hidden true
    box c2 "Hayek 1961: learned ≠ worthless" cites ["hayek1961"] x 330 y 310 width 560 height 66 radius 10 font_size 26 hidden true
    box c3 "Stigler & Becker 1977: stable tastes" cites ["stiglerbecker1977"] x 330 y 220 width 560 height 66 radius 10 font_size 26 hidden true
    portrait galbraith x 820 y 330 width 190 of "John Kenneth Galbraith" cameo true hidden true
    icon cig size 100 set twemoji x 760 y 330 icon_look picture of cigarette hidden true
    icon nosmoke size 100 set twemoji x 900 y 330 icon_look picture of "no smoking" hidden true
    text r1 "Leave tastes unjudged" x 350 y 380 font_size 30 hidden true
    text s1 "mostly agreed" #2f6b8f x 780 y 380 font_size 34 hidden true
    text r2 "Every choice is good for you" x 350 y 280 font_size 30 hidden true
    text s2 "contested" #b5482e x 780 y 280 font_size 34 hidden true
    cards sortc check each hidden true
        Assumed: People can rank options
        Not assumed: People are selfish
        Assumed: Choices reveal preferences
        Not assumed: Tastes are sensible, Money is all people want
    icon banana size 220 set twemoji x 500 y 360 icon_look picture of banana hidden true
    text rational "rational?" #2f6b8f x 500 y 220 font_size 44 hidden true

    card title "Who made the wants?"

Choices reveal rankings, so economists take want (@draw method respect parallel true@)s as given. But why refuse to judge them at all?

B: Two reasons. The first is met (@draw sub_method@) (@highlight method@)hod: wants are invisible and choices aren't, so choices are the data.

B: The second is respect: yo (@draw sub_respect@) (@highlight respect@)u, not an economist, are the best judge of your own life.

But where do wants come from? In 1958 John Kennet (@draw c1@)h Galbr (@draw galbraith@)aith argued that firms create, through advertising, the very wants they then satisfy.

If he is right, a choice reveals the advertiser' (@highlight c1@)s work as much as your own, and proves little about what is g (@erase galbraith@)ood for you.
    fade method sub_method to 0.3

B: Friedrich Hayek  (@draw c2@)replied in 1961: nearly every want beyond food and shelter is learned, a taste for music or novels too. Learn (@highlight c2@)ed isn't the same as worthless.

In 1977 George Stigler and  (@draw c3@)Gary Becker pushed the method the other way: assume tastes are stable and much the (@fade method sub_method to 1@) same in everyone, and expl (@highlight c3@)ain the differences by prices and experience.

B: A love of opera, they said, grows with listening. Not a new taste, but a skill built up, like capital.
    highlight c3

The newer challenge is behavioural. Say  (@draw cig nosmoke@)a smoker buys cigarettes and also pays for a course to quit. Which choice shows what he wants?

How sure are we? Leaving ta (@draw r1 s1@)stes unjudged as a working method: mostly agreed. Treating e (@draw r2 s2@)very choice as good for the chooser: contested.
    erase c1 c2 c3 cig nosmoke

So why do we buy what we buy? For reasons economics deliberately leaves unjudged, and reads only through the choices they leave behind.
    highlight method respect

    quiz "Say a bar sells $40 cubes of hand-carved ice for cocktails, and they sell out. What can an economist conclude?"
        right: Some buyers value a cube at $40 or more. Whether they should is not the model's call.
        wrong: The model reads the choice, not the reason behind it: here, some buyers value a cube at $40 or more.
        * The buyers are being fooled
        + Some buyers value a cube at $40 or more
        * Ice like this should be taxed

    quiz "Which finding would most trouble the idea that choices reveal what people want?"
        right: The default: same person, same options, different choice. Which one shows the real want?
        wrong: Look for a case where the choice changes but the person and the options do not: here, the default.
        * People buy more when a price falls
        + The same people choose differently when one option is made the default
        * Richer people buy more luxuries

    erase r1 s1 r2 s2 method respect sub_method sub_respect

    draw sortc

    ask "What does economics assume about people? Sort each card." on sortc stage own store f voice b
        right: All {f.total}. Only ranking and choices are assumed.
        wrong: {f} of {f.total} on the first try. Assumed: only ranking, and choices revealing it.

    erase sortc

B: Which raises a sharper question: if wants can (@draw banana rational@) be anything, even a banana on a wall, what could it mean to choose them rationally?

