    '|a||But where do wants come from? In 1958 John Kenneth Galbraith argued that firms create, through advertising, the very wants they then satisfy.':
      mp3: 
      ms: 9640
      voice: 'gemini:Achird | curious, open-minded economist host who loves a puzzle; warm, thinks aloud, brisk lively pace (never slow, never rushed); takes objections seriously, never preachy #ctx:cpqjqp'
    '|a||If he is right, a choice reveals the advertiser''s work as much as your own, and proves little about what is good for you.':
      mp3: 
      ms: 7720
      voice: 'gemini:Achird | curious, open-minded economist host who loves a puzzle; warm, thinks aloud, brisk lively pace (never slow, never rushed); takes objections seriously, never preachy #ctx:gttkbo'
    '|b||Friedrich Hayek replied in 1961: nearly every want beyond food and shelter is learned, a taste for music or novels too. Learned isn''t the same as worthless.':
      mp3: 
      ms: 11600
      voice: 'gemini:Kore | sharp, friendly co-host: asks what a smart listener would, pushes back with real objections, sometimes explains; dry wit, never exaggerated; brisk lively pace, never slow #ctx:1h99den'
    '|a||In 1977 George Stigler and Gary Becker pushed the method the other way: assume tastes are stable and much the same in everyone, and explain the differences by prices and experience.':
      mp3: 
      ms: 11800
      voice: 'gemini:Achird | curious, open-minded economist host who loves a puzzle; warm, thinks aloud, brisk lively pace (never slow, never rushed); takes objections seriously, never preachy #ctx:1vu0n4j'
    '|b||A love of opera, they said, grows with listening. Not a new taste, but a skill built up, like capital.':
      mp3: 
      ms: 7120
      voice: 'gemini:Kore | sharp, friendly co-host: asks what a smart listener would, pushes back with real objections, sometimes explains; dry wit, never exaggerated; brisk lively pace, never slow #ctx:17paops'
    '|a||The newer challenge is behavioural. Say a smoker buys cigarettes and also pays for a course to quit. Which choice shows what he wants?':
      mp3: 
      ms: 9840
      voice: 'gemini:Achird | curious, open-minded economist host who loves a puzzle; warm, thinks aloud, brisk lively pace (never slow, never rushed); takes objections seriously, never preachy #ctx:ed58o1'
    '|a||How sure are we? Leaving tastes unjudged as a working method: mostly agreed. Treating every choice as good for the chooser: contested.':
      mp3: 
      ms: 9720
      voice: 'gemini:Achird | curious, open-minded economist host who loves a puzzle; warm, thinks aloud, brisk lively pace (never slow, never rushed); takes objections seriously, never preachy #ctx:17eapx3'
    '|a||So why do we buy what we buy? For reasons economics deliberately leaves unjudged, and reads only through the choices they leave behind.':
      mp3: 
      ms: 8880
      voice: 'gemini:Achird | curious, open-minded economist host who loves a puzzle; warm, thinks aloud, brisk lively pace (never slow, never rushed); takes objections seriously, never preachy #ctx:1c8tr1o'
    '|a||Say a bar sells $40 cubes of hand-carved ice for cocktails, and they sell out. What can an economist conclude?':
      mp3: 
      ms: 8360
      voice: 'gemini:Achird | curious, open-minded economist host who loves a puzzle; warm, thinks aloud, brisk lively pace (never slow, never rushed); takes objections seriously, never preachy #ctx:1rv0ft5'
    '|a||Some buyers value a cube at $40 or more. Whether they should is not the model''s call.':
